{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "165",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her 50s with a history of recent hospitalization for congestive heart failure developed a painful ulceration on the right distal forearm at a previous peripheral intravenous catheter site. The ulceration appeared a few days after discharge. Recent activities included cleaning her fish tank, playing with her pet cat, and gardening in her indoor cactus garden. No recent travel history or immunosuppressive medications were reported. The patient was readmitted for bacterial pneumonia.\n\nThe patient had an ulcerated plaque on the right forearm with 2 tender, 1- to 2-cm red nodules in the right antecubital fossa.\n\n- Histopathologic examination (4-mm punch biopsy):  - Showed marked superficial and deep inflammation composed predominantly of neutrophils with associated tissue necrosis  - Multiple filamentous gram-positive bacilli identified on Gram stain  - Ziehl Nielson, Fite, and periodic acid–Schiff stains were negative for organisms  Images:  Image A Title: Ulcerated plaque on the right forearm  Image A Description: Shows ulcerated plaque and 2 tender, 1- to 2-cm red nodules in the right antecubital fossa    Image B Title: Histopathologic examination  Image B Description: Dense neutrophilic infiltrate with associated tissue necrosis (Hematoxylin-eosin, original magnification ×20)    Image C Title: Gram stain examination  Image C Description: Organisms seen on Gram stain (Original magnification ×60)  - Tissue culture: Positive for specific organism",
